PR #2883  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Adding a formally registered marchid for Raptor prevents processor misidentification — without unique (mvendorid,marchid) assignment, software may apply wrong security mitigations or errata workarounds, enabling privilege escape on the misidentified core.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/alternative.c:32-68  riscv_fill_cpu_mfr_info
      The (mvendorid, marchid) dispatch chain in _apply_alternatives() uses marchid to route CPU-specific errata patching. The switch statement dispatches on mvendorid alone, then each vendor's patch_func receives marchid and mimpid. This is architecturally sound per-vendor, but the correctness depends entirely on each vendor's marchid values being unique and properly registered. There is no cross-vendor marchid collision check. If a vendor's CPU reports an unregistered or conflicting marchid within the same mvendorid namespace, the wrong errata patch could be applied silently.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/errata/thead/errata.c:27-44  errata_probe_mae
      T-Head errata probe functions (mae, cmo, pmu, ghostwrite) all use the gating condition 'arch_id != 0 || impid != 0' to identify c9xx-class cores. This treats marchid==0 as a positive identification signal. Per the RISC-V privileged spec, marchid=0 is the 'unimplemented' default value. If a future T-Head-licensed implementation also reports marchid=0 and mimpid=0 but is a different microarchitecture with different errata requirements, the wrong errata workarounds would be applied — potentially disabling needed protections or enabling unsafe code paths. The comment clearly acknowledges 'target-c9xx cores report arch_id and impid as 0' but this is an implicit convention, not enforced by any hardware mechanism.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/cpufeature.c:891-902  riscv_fill_hwcap_from_isa_string
      The V-extension ambiguity heuristic checks 'boot_vendorid == THEAD_VENDOR_ID && boot_archid == 0x0' to decide that 'v' in the ISA string likely refers to the draft 0.7.1 vector spec rather than ratified V-1.0, and strips V support. The comment notes 'CPU cores with the ratified spec will contain non-zero marchid.' This relies on the unenforced assumption that all future T-Head cores implementing ratified V-1.0 will report a non-zero marchid. If a T-Head core with proper V-1.0 support reports marchid=0 (e.g., an SoC integrator forgets to set it), the kernel will silently disable V support on a compliant core, causing correct software to fail or fall back to scalar-only paths.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/main.c:20-28  kvm_riscv_setup_vendor_features
      The Andes AX66 detection uses a partial marchid match: '(riscv_cached_marchid(0) & 0xFFFF) == 0x8A66'. This only checks the lower 16 bits of the marchid, discarding the upper 48 bits. If another Andes microarchitecture shares the same lower 16 bits in its marchid but is architecturally different (e.g., an AX67 or future design reusing the low 16 encoding), it would be misidentified as AX66, and the VS-stage TLB GPA bypass would be incorrectly enabled. This could cause TLB coherency issues on a CPU that implements full GPA-tagged TLB lookups, leading to guest-to-guest or guest-to-host information leaks.
  [RISK] drivers/perf/riscv_pmu_sbi.c:1198-1206  pmu_sbi_setup_irqs
      T-Head PMU IRQ gating uses 'riscv_cached_marchid(0) == 0' combined with mvendorid==THEAD_VENDOR_ID and mimpid==0. Same issue as the errata probes: marchid==0 is used as a positive signal identifying c9xx-class cores. If a different T-Head microarchitecture also reports the (0x5b7, 0, 0) triple, it would get the T-Head-specific PMU IRQ number (THEAD_C9XX_RV_IRQ_PMU) instead of the standard SSCOFPMF IRQ, causing PMU interrupt misrouting — a functional issue with security implications if PMU overflow interrupts are used for kernel hardening features.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu.c:143-146  kvm_riscv_vcpu_setup
      VCPU setup copies the host marchid directly via 'vcpu->arch.marchid = sbi_get_marchid()'. The guest OS sees this value via SBI_EXT_BASE_GET_MARCHID. If a VM is migrated to a different physical host with a different marchid value, the guest OS's marchid-based security decisions become stale. The KVM one-reg interface (vcpu_onereg.c:412-418) allows userspace to override marchid before first VCPU run, but (a) there is no enforcement that the override matches the destination host during migration, and (b) the comment at vcpu_onereg.c:415-416 allows arbitrary marchid values to be injected by userspace without any validation against the host hardware. A malicious VMM could present a fake marchid to the guest, causing the guest to skip security mitigations it believes are unnecessary for the reported microarchitecture.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/cpu.c:195-218  riscv_cpuinfo_starting
      The marchid caching infrastructure (riscv_get_marchid, riscv_cached_marchid, riscv_cpuinfo_starting) correctly queries the marchid via SBI or M-mode CSR. The init-time path sets marchid unconditionally, and the hotplug path only sets it if the value is currently 0. The SBI v0.1 fallback to 0 is explicit and intentional. This is a straightforward, correct implementation of reading and caching the marchid value — no security-relevant decisions are made at this layer.
  [OK] arch/riscv/errata/sifive/errata.c:35-52  errata_cip_1200_check_func
      SiFive errata check functions use exact marchid constant matching (arch_id == 0x8000000000000007 or arch_id == 0x1) paired with mimpid range checks. This follows the RISC-V spec's intended pattern: registered, well-known marchid values with specific mimpid ranges define the affected microarchitectures. No ambiguous zero-value gating is used.
  [OK] arch/riscv/errata/andes/errata.c:42-63  errata_probe_iocp
      Andes errata checks use an exact marchid constant: ANDES_AX45MP_MARCHID (0x8000000000008a45UL) paired with a specific mimpid value. This is a registered, non-ambiguous marchid from a known vendor. The match is exact — no bitmasking or catchall zero-value gating. This follows the intended (mvendorid, marchid, mimpid) identification pattern correctly.
  [OK] arch/riscv/errata/mips/errata.c:36-67  mips_errata_patch_func
      MIPS errata patching does NOT use marchid for CPU identification at all. Instead, it probes for a vendor-specific ISA extension (XMIPSEXECTL) via riscv_isa_vendor_extension_available(). The marchid and impid parameters are accepted but entirely ignored. This is the safest approach — feature-based detection rather than ID-based matching — and fully compliant with the spec.

