PR #2883  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Adding a formally registered marchid for Raptor prevents processor misidentification — without unique (mvendorid,marchid) assignment, software may apply wrong security mitigations or errata workarounds, enabling privilege escape on the misidentified core.
  [OK] include/sbi/riscv_encoding.h:485-487  (macro definitions)
      These are the canonical CSR number constants CSR_MVENDORID=0xF11, CSR_MARCHID=0xF12, CSR_MIMPID=0xF13, matching the RISC-V privileged architecture specification. They are the authoritative CSR address defines and are correct. No logic or validation happens here — these are purely mechanical constant definitions.
  [OK] include/sbi/sbi_ecall_interface.h:47-49  (macro definitions)
      These are the SBI function ID constants for querying machine ID CSRs, matching the SBI specification v2.0. They are purely interface-definition constants with no logic, validation, or control flow. Risk only exists downstream in the handler that implements these function IDs.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_ecall_base.c:55-63  sbi_ecall_base_handler
      The SBI base handler reads CSR_MVENDORID, CSR_MARCHID, and CSR_MIMPID directly via csr_read() and returns the raw hardware values verbatim to S-mode software. There is no whitelist, registration table, or any validation of the (mvendorid, marchid) tuple. If a vendor ships silicon with an unregistered or conflicting marchid value, the firmware faithfully propagates a misleading identification triple to the Linux kernel. This relies on the implicit, unenforced invariant that all supported hardware uses properly registered (mvendorid, marchid) pairs from the JEDEC/RISC-V International registry.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_ecall_vendor.c:18-23  sbi_ecall_vendor_id
      Computes the vendor-specific SBI extension-ID range as SBI_EXT_VENDOR_START + (mvendorid masked to 16Mi). The vendor extension-ID space (0x09000000–0x09FFFFFF) is only 16Mi, so any mvendorid collision causes extension-ID collision. RISC-V International assigns mvendorid values from the JEDEC manufacturer ID space, which in principle prevents collisions, but this code does not validate or cross-check the mvendorid against any known-good table. If an unregistered vendor reuses an existing JEDEC ID, two vendors' SBI vendor extensions will occupy the same extension range, and the wrong vendor_ext_provider will be invoked.
  [RISK] platform/generic/include/andes/andes.h:73-77  is_andes
      The is_andes(series) macro identifies the CPU series by reading CSR_MARCHID alone — it does NOT read CSR_MVENDORID to first confirm the vendor is Andes. The macro extracts the major (bits 7:4) and minor (bits 3:0) version from marchid and compares against the decoded series number. A non-Andes vendor whose marchid happens to encode a matching major/minor pattern (e.g. any marchid where (value>>4)*10+(value&0x0f) == 25) would be misidentified as AX25. This means Andes-specific cache-coherency, PMA, and power-management code paths could be activated on completely unrelated hardware. The code relies on the implicit, unenforced invariant that the platform config only builds for Andes hardware.
  [RISK] lib/utils/hsm/fdt_hsm_andes_atcsmu.c:127-128  ae350_hart_start
      Calls is_andes(25) to identify AX25MP and skip IPI wakeup for hart0 (which shares a power domain with the LLC). If an ambiguous or colliding marchid causes is_andes(25) to return true on non-Andes hardware, the IPI wakeup path is bypassed for hart0 on a processor where that is inappropriate — the hart will not be woken. Conversely, a false negative on genuine AX25 hardware would attempt to power-cycle the LLC-coupled hart0, potentially crashing the system. The root cause is the same missing mvendorid guard in is_andes().
  [RISK] lib/utils/hsm/fdt_hsm_andes_atcsmu.c:146-147  ae350_hart_stop
      Calls is_andes(25) to identify AX25MP and return SBI_ENOTSUPP for hart0 stop (refusing to power down the LLC-coupled hart). Same vulnerability as the hart_start path: a false marchid match from another vendor would prevent hart0 sleep on hardware that should support it. A false negative on genuine AX25 would allow an unsafe power-down via WFI, leaving the LLC without power. Again, the root cause is the missing mvendorid check: is_andes() uses marchid alone without confirming the vendor first.

