PR #2883  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Adding a formally registered marchid for Raptor prevents processor misidentification — without unique (mvendorid,marchid) assignment, software may apply wrong security mitigations or errata workarounds, enabling privilege escape on the misidentified core.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/riscv/locore.S:283-303  get_pte_fixup_bits
      Bootstrap-phase PTE attribute fixup checks ONLY mvendorid==T_HEAD (via SBI call), with ZERO marchid validation. If a CPU reports MVENDORID_THEAD but uses standard Svpbmt PTE encoding (e.g. a future T-Head core, or a malicious firmware misreport), the function sets PTE_THEAD_MA_NONE as the fixup bit, constructing bootstrap page tables with incompatible memory attributes. The kernel never reaches identify_cpu() to correct this. The code's own comment at lines 284-290 explicitly acknowledges this is 'inconvenient and fragile'. No fence or error check exists.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/pmap.c:940-950  pmap_bootstrap
      Selects PTE memory attribute bits for all runtime page tables based on has_svpbmt vs has_errata_thead_pbmt flags. has_errata_thead_pbmt is set unconditionally for all T-Head CPUs in handle_thead_quirks (mvendorid-only dispatch). If marchid misidentification causes the wrong flag to be set, all subsequent page table entries get incorrect memory ordering/caching attributes — affecting DMA coherence, device MMIO, and non-cacheable memory. The correctness depends on the unenforced invariant that all CPUs reporting MVENDORID_THEAD use the T-Head PTE encoding rather than standard Svpbmt.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/identcpu.c:517-537  handle_thead_quirks
